// Detective: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== detective.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Invalid's Sister
genre: detective + thriller
tone: Gaslit, watchful and quietly audacious
era: 1861 | period
setting: A slave-holding port city and the night railway north of it | fictional | city
logline: The first woman ever hired by a private detective agency goes south as a society flirt to learn whether a president-elect is to be murdered changing trains, and then has to smuggle him through the city in a sleeping-car berth as her sick brother.
premise: Five years ago {kate}, a widow of twenty-three, walked into a detective agency and asked for work. The proprietor assumed she wanted to be a clerk. She explained that a woman could befriend the wives and sweethearts of criminals and go where no man could. Now a railway hires the agency to investigate threats to its bridges, and the threads lead to something larger: a plot to kill the incoming president where his route crosses a hostile city on foot.
synopsis: The first act is a disguise worn in public. {kate} arrives at the best hotel with a southern accent, a secessionist cockade and a gift for listening. Drawing rooms are the film's interrogation cells; the men boast to impress her, the women confide. Her chief, {the_principal}, works the bars under another name. They compare notes on a park bench as strangers. What they assemble: a barber-captain, {corvino}, who has sworn to do it; a ballot of red cards; a crowd arranged to distract the police; a narrow passage between two stations. The second act is persuading the target. The president-elect's own circle thinks stealing through a city by night is cowardice and bad politics. {kate}'s case is made of gossip, and she knows how that sounds. She is the one who points out that the evidence is exactly as good as the access, and that nobody else had hers. The agreed plan is hers in its domestic detail: he leaves a dinner early; telegraph wires out of the city are cut for the night; she books the rear half of a sleeping car for herself and an invalid brother who must not be disturbed. The third act is one night, nearly in real time. A tall man in a soft hat and a shawl stoops to pass as a sick man on her arm. She tips the conductor as a worried sister would. The car is uncoupled and drawn by horses through the sleeping city at half past three, past the very passage. She sits by the berth with a pistol in her muff and does not sleep; the agency will later put an open eye on its letterhead. At dawn, a telegram in code: "Plums delivered nuts safely." The newspapers mock him for sneaking in. {corvino} is never tried, there being no crime. {kate} goes back to work. The film ends on her name in the agency's ledger, with the word "superintendent" beside it, and an annotation that no likeness of her is known for certain.
question: How do you prove a crime that your own work has prevented?
themes: access as intelligence; the undervalued witness; a success that looks like nothing happening
audience: Adults and older teenagers; lovers of period suspense
viewpoint: With {kate}; the famous passenger is seen only as a stooped back, a hand, a voice in the dark
stakes: A president's life on the eve of a civil war, and the standing of every woman who will ever want this job
conflict: A detective whose evidence is talk against conspirators who talk too much, and against allies who would rather be brave than careful
ending: He passes through in her care, asleep or pretending. Nothing happens. That is the triumph, and it is never quite believed.
experience: Parlour suspense, a long held breath through a dark city, and the dry satisfaction of a job done unseen
look: Hotel gaslight, crinolines and cockades; a sleeping car's curtained berths; empty streets with a single horse-drawn carriage on rails
sound: Piano in the next room, telegraph keys, then silence where a wire is cut. Hooves and iron wheels at half past three.
m: protect | secret | faith-vs-evidence | triumph
@ kate | Kate Warner | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 28 | To get one man across one city | She argued her way into this trade and has to be right | Her proof is drawing-room talk | Cool, observant, a superb listener, privately funny | From operative to the one whose plan it is | A borrowed southern drawl; her own voice is plain and northern | Travelling dress, a cockade, a muff with a pistol in it
@ the_principal | The Principal | co-lead | mentor | human | he/him | 41 | To deliver the client and keep the agency's name | He hired her against all advice | He must convince men who dislike detectives | Blunt, dogged, proud of her | From employer to colleague taking her instructions | Scots burr, short orders | Bowler, a beard, a habit of not sleeping
@ corvino | Captain Corvino | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 39 | To be the man who did it | He has said so in too many bars | He cannot resist an admiring woman | Vain, theatrical, sincere | From boaster to a man waiting at the wrong station | Flourishes, oaths | Barber's hands, a militia sash, a red card
@ the_passenger | The Passenger | supporting | none | human | he/him | 52 | To arrive, and not to look afraid | A country is splitting | The safe course looks like cowardice | Wry, patient, long-limbed | From reluctance to trust | Dry frontier humour, in a whisper | A soft hat, a shawl, boots too long for the berth
~ kate - the_principal | operative and chief | an experiment he is defending | a plan of hers he carries out
~ kate - corvino | admirer and boaster | flattery | he never learns who she was
~ kate - the_passenger | "sister" and "invalid brother" | a parcel to be delivered | a joke shared in the dark, and a hand shaken
# The Hotel | INT | Hotel Drawing Room | NIGHT | kate, corvino | Listen | A cockade, a drawl, a fan. The captain shows off: there are men who will see he never reaches the capital. She says she does not believe him. He explains | The plot is confirmed | 6 | crowd=60, props=cockade+fan
# The Hotel | EXT | Park Bench | DAY | kate, the_principal | Compare notes | Two strangers and a newspaper. Red cards, a passage between stations, a crowd laid on. He asks how sure. She says: as sure as gossip | | 5 | props=newspaper
# The Case | INT | Hotel Suite, Another City | NIGHT | kate, the_principal, the_passenger | Persuade him | His people call it skulking. She sets out what she heard and from whom, and what it would take to be wrong. A long pause; a long man says he will go as her brother | He agrees to the plan | 6 | crowd=8
# The Case | INT | Railway Booking Office | DAY | kate | Book the berths | The rear half of the last sleeping car, for a lady and an invalid. She fusses about draughts. She tips | | 4 | props=tickets
# The Night | EXT | Station Platform, Rear Door | NIGHT | kate, the_passenger, the_principal | Board | A stooped man in a shawl on her arm. A conductor waved past by a worried sister. Somewhere a lineman cuts a wire | | 5 | vehicles=period sleeping car, crowd=20
# The Night | INT | Sleeping Car, Curtained Berth | NIGHT | kate, the_passenger | Cross the city | Half past three. The car is drawn by horses through the streets. The passage goes by the window. She has a pistol in her muff. He makes one joke, very quietly | They pass the place | 7 | vehicles=horse-drawn rail car, animals=horses, props=pistol+muff
# The Night | INT | Telegraph Office | DAWN | the_principal, kate | Send the code | "Plums delivered nuts safely." He laughs for the first time. She is already asleep in a chair | The passenger arrives | 4 | props=telegraph
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from a railway journey of February 1861 and the first woman detective, with invented names
